  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through Friday)...\n\nZonal flow prevails over our region today behind last nights\nweather system. Look for mostly clear skies and highs topping out\nin the 40s to lower 50s. Clear skies and light winds will lead to\na chilly autumn night with lows mainly in the 20s. \n\nLate Sunday into Monday, an upper low currently resting over the \ndesert SW, will drift across the central Rockies. Confidence is \nincreasing thats its track will cross through Wyoming into the \nDakotas by Monday night. This is a favorable track for SE MT. \nThus, the chance of precipitation over southeast Montana is \ntrending upward with PoPs now ranging from 40-80% mainly Sunday \nevening through Monday. As temperatures decrease overnight, rain \nmay change over to snow showers into Monday, although little to no\nsnow accumulation is expected. There remains less than 20% chance\nof more than a trace of snow outside of the higher hills. This \ndoes not look like a significant precip producer for the \neast...with most locations totaling under 0.10 inches of liquid.\n\nIn the meantime, easterly low level flow increases over the plains \nand this upslope flow may result in areas of fog and low clouds\nacross our western foothills and nearby plains late Sunday night\ninto early Monday. \n\nA stronger upper trough is progged to aggressively push into the \nnorthern Rockies early this week, impacting our region later Monday\nnight through Tuesday night. The exact track of the associated \njet streak will decide where the best upper dynamics hit, but at \nthis time our PoPs reflect a decent widespread precipitation event\n(40-70% over the plains, 60-90% over the mountains). The chance of\nat least 0.25 ins of liquid ranges from 65% in the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth Mtns to 20-40% over portions of the plains. \nWhile highs on Tuesday are expected to be in the 40s to lower 50s,\nwe should see some snow in the high country with 2-4 inches above\n7000 ft. Details may change, so stay informed if you have travel \nor outdoor plans to start the week. \n\nFor the rest of the work week, while there does not appear to be \nany organized weather systems affecting our region, southwest flow\naloft prevails which may result in some unsettled weather, \nparticularly for the mountains. Temps remain around or perhaps a \nlittle under seasonable averages. BT",
